---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CompressTool from '../components/CompressTool.astro';
const title = 'Compress Image to 200KB Online - Free | CompressImg';
const description = 'Compress JPG, PNG and WebP images to 200KB or less for free, no signup. Keep blog images, product photos and site graphics sharp while your pages load fast.';
const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'How to compress image to 200KB?',
    answer: 'Upload your image with the tool pre-set to 200KB. It optimizes the file and, if necessary, refines quality and dimensions until the result is at or below 200KB.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Is 200KB good for website images?',
    answer: 'A 200KB limit can work well for prominent website images such as product photos and featured blog images. Decorative images and thumbnails often benefit from a smaller target.'
  }
];
---
<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical="/compress-image-to-200kb" schemaType="WebApplication" faqItems={faqItems}>
      <section class="border-b border-stone-200 py-16 md:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <p class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-stone-200 bg-stone-100 px-3.5 py-1.5 font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-stone-600">
        <span class="h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-accent-500"></span>
        Compress to 200KB
      </p>
      <h1 class="mt-6 text-4xl font-semibold leading-[1.08] tracking-tightest text-stone-900 md:text-5xl">Compress image to <span class="text-accent-700">200KB</span> online - free</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[60ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-stone-600 md:text-lg">Reduce your image to 200KB with excellent quality retention. Perfect for web graphics, blog images, and high-quality uploads. Supports JPG, PNG, and WebP.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 -mt-2 mb-12">
    <CompressTool defaultTarget={200} showCustomTarget={true} />
  </section>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pb-20 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <h2 class="mb-5 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight text-stone-900">Other compression sizes</h2>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
      <a href="/compress-image-to-20kb" class="rounded-full border border-stone-200 bg-white px-4 py-2 font-mono text-sm font-medium text-stone-600 transition duration-200 hover:border-accent-600 hover:text-accent-700 active:scale-[0.97]">Compress to 20KB</a>
      <a href="/compress-image-to-30kb" class="rounded-full border border-stone-200 bg-white px-4 py-2 font-mono text-sm font-medium text-stone-600 transition duration-200 hover:border-accent-600 hover:text-accent-700 active:scale-[0.97]">Compress to 30KB</a>
      <a href="/compress-image-to-50kb" class="rounded-full border border-stone-200 bg-white px-4 py-2 font-mono text-sm font-medium text-stone-600 transition duration-200 hover:border-accent-600 hover:text-accent-700 active:scale-[0.97]">Compress to 50KB</a>
      <a href="/compress-image-to-100kb" class="rounded-full border border-stone-200 bg-white px-4 py-2 font-mono text-sm font-medium text-stone-600 transition duration-200 hover:border-accent-600 hover:text-accent-700 active:scale-[0.97]">Compress to 100KB</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-stone-900">Why Compress Images to 200KB?</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-600">The 200KB target size is ideal when you need high-quality images that still load quickly. Unlike the more aggressive 20KB or 50KB compressions, 200KB gives the compression algorithm much more room to work with, resulting in images that retain excellent visual quality. This makes 200KB the sweet spot for website hero images, blog post featured images, product photos on e-commerce sites, and portfolio galleries where visual impression matters.</p>
    <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-600">Web performance experts often cite 200KB as a good benchmark for the largest images on a webpage. By keeping your main images at or under 200KB, you ensure fast page load times while maintaining the visual quality that engages visitors. This is particularly important for image-heavy websites like photography portfolios, travel blogs, food websites, and online stores where images are the primary content.</p>
    <h2 class="mb-4 mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-stone-900">200KB Image Quality Expectations</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-600">At the 200KB target, you can expect excellent results for most images. A typical photograph at 1200x800 to 1600x1200 pixels will look nearly indistinguishable from the original when compressed to 200KB. The JPEG quality setting will typically be in the 70-90% range at this size, which preserves virtually all visible detail. Even images with fine textures, subtle gradients, and complex patterns will retain good fidelity at 200KB.</p>
    <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-600">For web designers and content creators, 200KB images provide the best balance of quality and performance. You can use them as full-width hero images, detailed product shots, and featured blog images without worrying about pixelation or compression artifacts. The slightly larger file size is a worthwhile trade-off for the significantly improved visual appeal compared to more aggressively compressed alternatives.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="border-t border-stone-200 bg-white py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="mb-10 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-stone-900 md:text-3xl">Frequently Asked Questions - 200KB Compression</h2>
      <div class="divide-y divide-stone-200 border-y border-stone-200">
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn flex w-full items-center justify-between gap-4 py-5 text-left text-[15px] font-medium text-stone-900 transition-colors hover:text-accent-700">
            How to compress image to 200KB?
            <svg class="faq-icon h-4 w-4 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 transition-transform duration-300 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.32,0.72,0,1)]" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-content hidden pb-5">
            <p class="max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-stone-600">Upload your image with the tool pre-set to 200KB. It optimizes the file and, if necessary, refines quality and dimensions until the result is at or below 200KB.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-btn flex w-full items-center justify-between gap-4 py-5 text-left text-[15px] font-medium text-stone-900 transition-colors hover:text-accent-700">
            Is 200KB good for website images?
            <svg class="faq-icon h-4 w-4 flex-shrink-0 text-stone-400 transition-transform duration-300 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.32,0.72,0,1)]" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-content hidden pb-5">
            <p class="max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-stone-600">A 200KB limit can work well for prominent website images such as product photos and featured blog images. Decorative images and thumbnails often benefit from a smaller target.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
<script>
  document.querySelectorAll('.faq-btn').forEach(btn => {
    btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const content = btn.nextElementSibling;
      const icon = btn.querySelector('.faq-icon');
      content.classList.toggle('hidden');
      icon.classList.toggle('rotate-180');
    });
  });
</script>
